(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the hand-over-hand processive motility mechanism of Kinesin-1 along a microtubule protofilament.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Processive Motility Mechanism of Conventional Kinesin-1:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   Kinesin-1 is a heterotetramer consisting of two heavy chains and two light chains. Each heavy) Tj
T*
(   chain forms a globular motor head \(catalytic ATPase and microtubule-binding domain\), a flexible) Tj
T*
(   neck linker \(~15 amino acids\), an extended coiled-coil stalk, and a cargo-binding tail.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Hand-Over-Hand Stepping Cycle:) Tj
T*
(   Kinesin moves processively toward the microtubule plus-end, taking over 100 consecutive 8 nm) Tj
T*
(   steps \(the exact longitudinal repeat of an alpha-beta tubulin dimer\) before detaching:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1 \(Leading Head Binds ATP\): Both heads are bound to adjacent tubulin dimers. The rear) Tj
T*
(   trailing head has bound ADP, while the forward leading head is nucleotide-free. Binding of ATP) Tj
T*
(   to the leading head induces a conformational docking of its neck linker against the catalytic) Tj
T*
(   core, throwing the trailing head forward by 16 nm.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2 \(Trailing Head Steps Forward\): The forward-swung head binds the next tubulin dimer 8) Tj
T*
(   nm ahead of the partner head, becoming the new leading head.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3 \(ATP Hydrolysis and Coordination\): ATP in the trailing head is hydrolyzed to ADP + Pi.) Tj
T*
(   Concurrently, the leading head releases its bound ADP.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4 \(Phosphate Release\): Release of inorganic phosphate from the rear head loosens its) Tj
T*
(   affinity for the microtubule, priming it to detach and step forward during the subsequent) Tj
T*
(   cycle.) Tj
T*
(   3. Strict 1:1 Mechanochemical Coupling:) Tj
T*
(   Optical trap experiments demonstrate that kinesin hydrolyzes exactly 1 ATP molecule for every 8) Tj
T*
(   nm step, generating stall forces up to 6-7 pN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular mechanism of Actin Treadmilling, deriving the steady-state conditions using critical concentrations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Kinetics and Thermodynamics of Actin Treadmilling:) Tj
T*
(   1. Critical Concentration \(Cc\):) Tj
T*
(   The critical concentration is the monomeric G-actin concentration at which the rate of subunit) Tj
T*
(   addition \(k_on * [G-actin]\) precisely balances the rate of subunit loss \(k_off\):) Tj
T*
(   Cc = k_off / k_on = 1 / K_eq.) Tj
T*
(   If [G-actin] > Cc, filaments elongate; if [G-actin] < Cc, filaments depolymerize.) Tj
T*
(   2. Kinetic Asymmetry between Barbed \(+\) and Pointed \(-\) Ends:) Tj
T*
(   Because ATP hydrolysis accompanies polymerization, the structural states at opposing ends are) Tj
T*
(   non-equivalent:) Tj
T*
(   - Barbed \(+\) End: Rapid subunit exchange. ATP-G-actin binds with high on-rate: Cc\(+\) is) Tj
T*
(   approximately 0.12 microM.) Tj
T*
(   - Pointed \(-\) End: Slow subunit exchange. ADP-G-actin dissociates with low affinity: Cc\(-\) is) Tj
T*
(   approximately 0.60 microM.) Tj
T*
(   3. Treadmilling Steady-State Condition:) Tj
T*
(   When the soluble G-actin concentration [G] falls in the intermediate regime:) Tj
T*
(   Cc\(+\) < [G] < Cc\(-\) \(e.g., [G] = 0.35 microM\):) Tj
T*
(   - At the barbed end: [G] > Cc\(+\), so net polymerization occurs \(subunits continuously add\).) Tj
T*
ET

