(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the Arp2/3 complex nucleation mechanism and explain how it drives branched actin networks at lamellipodia.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Arp2/3 Complex and Branched Actin Network Polymerization:) Tj
T*
(   1. Architecture of Arp2/3 Complex:) Tj
T*
(   A stable seven-subunit macromolecular complex consisting of two Actin-Related Proteins \(Arp2) Tj
T*
(   and Arp3\) and five structural subunits \(ARPC1 to ARPC5\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Activation by Nucleation Promoting Factors \(NPFs\):) Tj
T*
(   In the basal state, Arp2 and Arp3 are held in an inactive, separated conformation:) Tj
T*
(   - Extracellular migratory cues activate the Rho-family GTPase Rac1, which recruits the) Tj
T*
(   WAVE/SCAR regulatory complex to the leading edge membrane.) Tj
T*
(   - Alternatively, active Cdc42 binds and relieves auto-inhibition of N-WASP.) Tj
T*
(   - The C-terminal WCA domain \(WH2, Central, Acidic\) of WASP/WAVE binds monomeric G-actin via the) Tj
T*
(   WH2 domain and binds Arp2/3 via the CA domain.) Tj
T*
(   - This delivers G-actin and forces Arp2 and Arp3 into close proximity, perfectly mimicking an) Tj
T*
(   actin dimer.) Tj
T*
(   3. Daughter Branch Nucleation:) Tj
T*
(   The activated Arp2/3 complex docks against the lateral side of a pre-existing actin mother) Tj
T*
(   filament. Elongation proceeds from the Arp2/Arp3 pseudo-dimer, nucleating a new daughter) Tj
T*
(   filament that grows outward at a stereotypic 70-degree angle.) Tj
T*
(   4. Lamellipodial Protrusion:) Tj
T*
(   Rapid barbed-end polymerization of the branched 70-degree dendritic actin meshwork directly) Tj
T*
(   pushes against the plasma membrane, generating protrusion forces \(1-3 nN/micron^2\) driving) Tj
T*
(   cellular migration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the cross-bridge cycle of Skeletal Muscle Myosin II and detail the structural triggers of the power stroke.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Lymn-Taylor Mechanochemical Cross-Bridge Cycle of Myosin II:) Tj
T*
(   1. State 1 \(Rigor State - Attached\):) Tj
T*
(   In the absence of ATP, the myosin head is tightly locked to an actin filament at a 45-degree) Tj
T*
(   angle.) Tj
T*
(   2. State 2 \(ATP Binding and Detachment\):) Tj
T*
(   ATP binds to the nucleotide-binding pocket on the myosin head. This causes an immediate) Tj
T*
(   conformational closure of the active site cleft that disrupts the actin-binding surface,) Tj
T*
(   reducing actin affinity by 10,000-fold. The myosin head detaches from actin.) Tj
T*
(   3. State 3 \(ATP Hydrolysis and Cocking\):) Tj
T*
(   Myosin hydrolyzes ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate \(Pi\), both of which remain trapped in the) Tj
T*
(   catalytic pocket. Hydrolysis triggers a large 60-degree swing of the converter domain and) Tj
T*
(   alpha-helical lever arm, repositioning \('cocking'\) the head ~11 nm forward into a) Tj
T*
(   pre-power-stroke conformation.) Tj
T*
(   4. State 4 \(Weak Actin Binding\):) Tj
T*
ET

