(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the retrieval mechanism of ER-resident proteins from the Golgi via the KDEL receptor and COP I retrograde transport.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   ER Protein Retrieval and COP I Retrograde Trafficking:) Tj
T*
(   1. Signal Sequences for ER Retention:) Tj
T*
(   Soluble luminal ER proteins \(e.g., BiP/GRP78, Protein Disulfide Isomerase - PDI, Calreticulin\)) Tj
T*
(   terminate with a carboxy-terminal tetrapeptide signal: Lys-Asp-Glu-Leu \(KDEL\).) Tj
T*
(   Transmembrane ER proteins possess a C-terminal dilysine motif \(KKXX or KXKXX\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Escape and Recognition in Golgi Cisternae:) Tj
T*
(   During bulk-flow transport, some ER resident proteins are non-specifically swept forward in COP) Tj
T*
(   II vesicles to the ER-Golgi Intermediate Compartment \(ERGIC\) and cis-Golgi.) Tj
T*
(   In the cis-Golgi, the seven-transmembrane KDEL receptor \(ERD2\) captures escaped KDEL proteins) Tj
T*
(   with high nanomolar affinity.) Tj
T*
(   3. pH-Dependent Affinity Regulation:) Tj
T*
(   The binding affinity of the KDEL receptor is strictly pH-regulated:) Tj
T*
(   - In the cis-Golgi \(pH ~6.2 to 6.4\), protonation of critical histidine residues in the binding) Tj
T*
(   pocket stabilizes electrostatic interactions with the KDEL carboxylate, driving tight ligand) Tj
T*
(   binding.) Tj
T*
(   - In the ER lumen \(pH ~7.2 to 7.4\), deprotonation causes allosteric opening and rapid release) Tj
T*
(   of the escaped proteins into the ER lumen.) Tj
T*
(   4. COP I Coat Recruitment:) Tj
T*
(   Ligand-bound KDEL receptor exposes a cytosolic dilysine-like motif that recruits the) Tj
T*
(   pre-assembled heptameric coatomer complex \(alpha, beta, beta', gamma, delta, epsilon, zeta) Tj
T*
(   subunits\) and Arf1-GTP.) Tj
T*
(   COP I polymerizes into a retrograde transport vesicle, transporting KDEL cargo and receptors) Tj
T*
(   back to the ER.) Tj
T*
