T*
(   2. Sorting in the Trans-Golgi Network \(TGN\):) Tj
T*
(   In the TGN, transmembrane M6P Receptors \(MPRs: cation-dependent CD-MPR and cation-independent) Tj
T*
(   CI-MPR\) bind M6P residues at pH 6.5. The cytosolic tails of MPRs recruit GGA adaptors and) Tj
T*
(   AP-1/clathrin coats, packaging hydrolases into transport vesicles targeted to late endosomes.) Tj
T*
(   3. Release in Endosomes and Receptor Recycling:) Tj
T*
(   Upon fusing with late endosomes, the acidic pH \(~5.0 to 5.5\) triggers hydrolase dissociation) Tj
T*
(   from MPRs. Acid phosphatases dephosphorylate the M6P tag, preventing rebinding. The retromer) Tj
T*
(   complex captures empty MPRs and returns them back to the TGN.) Tj
T*
(   4. Molecular Pathology of I-Cell Disease \(Mucolipidosis II\):) Tj
T*
(   Caused by autosomal recessive mutations in the GNPTAB gene encoding the catalytic subunit of) Tj
T*
(   GlcNAc phosphotransferase:) Tj
T*
(   - Without M6P tags, newly synthesized acid hydrolases cannot bind MPRs. Instead, they are) Tj
T*
(   packaged into constitutive secretory vesicles and dumped into the bloodstream and extracellular) Tj
T*
(   fluids.) Tj
T*
(   - Lysosomes become severely deficient in digestive enzymes, accumulating undigested) Tj
T*
(   glycosaminoglycans, lipids, and oligosaccharides as massive dense 'inclusion bodies' \(I-cells\),) Tj
T*
(   causing severe skeletal deformities, coarse facial features, and early childhood mortality.) Tj
