(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the biochemical structure, assembly, and tissue distribution of Intermediate Filaments, contrasting acidic/basic keratins, vimentin, desmin, and neurofilaments.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Intermediate Filament Classification, Assembly, and Distribution:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conserved Subunit Domain Structure:) Tj
T*
(   All intermediate filament polypeptides possess a central, conserved alpha-helical rod domain) Tj
T*
(   \(~310-350 amino acids\) containing four coiled-coil segments \(1A, 1B, 2A, 2B\) separated by) Tj
T*
(   flexible linkers \(L1, L12, L2\), flanked by variable non-helical N-terminal 'head' and) Tj
T*
(   C-terminal 'tail' domains.) Tj
T*
(   2. Stepwise Hierarchical Assembly:) Tj
T*
(   - Dimer Formation: Two parallel monomers associate side-by-side to form a coiled-coil dimer.) Tj
T*
(   - Tetramer Formation: Two dimers align in an antiparallel, half-staggered orientation to form a) Tj
T*
(   symmetrical tetramer. Because ends are structurally identical, tetramers lack polarity!) Tj
T*
