(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular pathogenesis of Epidermolysis Bullosa Simplex \(EBS\) and its relationship to Keratin 5 and Keratin 14 mutations.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Pathogenesis of Epidermolysis Bullosa Simplex \(EBS\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Normal Function of Keratin 5/14 in Basal Keratinocytes:) Tj
T*
(   The basal layer of the stratified squamous epidermis expresses high levels of Keratin 5 \(Type) Tj
T*
(   II\) and Keratin 14 \(Type I\). These pair into robust heterodimeric keratin intermediate filament) Tj
T*
(   bundles that anchor into hemidesmosomes basally and desmosomes laterally, forming a continuous) Tj
T*
(   mechanical scaffold that resists frictional shear stress.) Tj
T*
(   2. Genetic Mutation Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   EBS is predominantly an autosomal dominant genodermatosis caused by heterozygous missense) Tj
T*
(   mutations in either KRT5 or KRT14 genes:) Tj
T*
(   - Over 75% of pathogenic mutations cluster within the conserved boundary regions of the) Tj
T*
(   alpha-helical rod domain, specifically the Helix Initiation Motif \(H1\) and Helix Termination) Tj
T*
(   Motif \(HT\).) Tj
T*
(   - A classic example is the mutation p.Arg125Cys in KRT14.) Tj
T*
(   - Mutations act via a dominant-negative mechanism: the mutant polypeptide incorporates into) Tj
T*
(   nascent keratin dimers, but disrupts the critical leucine-zipper and electrostatic interactions) Tj
T*
(   required for lateral tetramer packing.) Tj
T*
(   3. Cellular and Clinical Pathology:) Tj
T*
(   - The defective keratin network cannot assemble into continuous filaments; instead, it) Tj
T*
(   collapses into non-functional protein aggregates.) Tj
T*
(   - When mild frictional or mechanical shear stress is applied to the skin, basal keratinocytes) Tj
T*
(   lack structural integrity and lyse horizontally through the cytoplasm \(intraepidermal blister) Tj
T*
(   cleavage plane\).) Tj
T*
(   - This leads to painful fluid-filled blisters and skin erosions, predominantly on palms, soles,) Tj
T*
(   and points of friction.) Tj
T*
