(   [Problem Statement]: Explain how bacterial pathogens like Listeria and Shigella utilize host cell actin polymerization for intracellular motility and cell-to-cell spread.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Pathogenic Exploitation of Host Actin Polymerization:) Tj
T*
(   1. Intracellular Invasion and Escape:) Tj
T*
(   Pathogens such as Listeria monocytogenes and Shigella flexneri are ingested by host cells into) Tj
T*
(   phagosomes, from which they rapidly escape into the cytoplasm via pore-forming hemolysins) Tj
T*
(   \(Listeriolysin O for Listeria; IpaB/IpaC for Shigella\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Molecular Mimicry and Actin Nucleation:) Tj
T*
(   - Listeria ActA Protein: Expressed asymmetrically at the bacterial posterior pole. ActA) Tj
T*
(   possesses an N-terminal domain containing a central/acidic \(CA\) motif mimicking host WASP, and) Tj
T*
(   an amphipathic actin-binding region. ActA directly binds and activates the host Arp2/3 complex.) Tj
T*
(   - Shigella IcsA \(VirG\) Protein: Expressed at the old bacterial pole; directly recruits host) Tj
T*
(   N-WASP, which in turn recruits Arp2/3.) Tj
T*
(   3. Actin Comet Tail Assembly and Propulsion:) Tj
T*
(   - Active Arp2/3 nucleates a dense, branched F-actin network against the bacterial pole.) Tj
T*
(   - Rapid addition of ATP-actin monomers at barbed ends pushes the bacterium forward at) Tj
T*
(   velocities up to 1.5 microns/second.) Tj
T*
(   - Cofilin accelerates pointed-end depolymerization of older filaments at the rear of the tail,) Tj
T*
(   recycling G-actin monomers to sustain continuous forward propulsion.) Tj
T*
(   4. Paracellular Spread:) Tj
T*
(   Propelled bacteria reach the plasma membrane and push outward into long filopodia-like) Tj
T*
(   protrusions \('listeriopods'\) that are engulfed by neighboring host cells via endocytosis,) Tj
T*
(   enabling pathogen dissemination throughout the tissue while remaining completely shielded from) Tj
T*
(   extracellular antibodies and complement.) Tj
T*
