(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular mechanism of Phagocytosis, detailing Fc-receptor recognition, pseudopod extension, and phagolysosome maturation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Molecular Stages of Phagocytosis and Phagolysosomal Maturation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Target Recognition and Opsonization:) Tj
T*
(   Phagocytes \(macrophages, neutrophils, dendritic cells\) ingest large particles \(> 0.5 microns,) Tj
T*
(   such as bacterial pathogens or apoptotic corpses\). Pathogens opsonized with IgG antibodies are) Tj
T*
(   recognized by cell-surface Fc-gamma receptors \(Fc-gamma-R\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Receptor Clustering and Signal Transduction:) Tj
T*
(   Multivalent ligand binding clusters Fc-gamma-R, activating receptor-associated Src-family) Tj
T*
(   kinases \(Lyn, Hck, Fgr\) that phosphorylate Immunoreceptor Tyrosine-based Activation Motifs) Tj
T*
(   \(ITAMs\). Phosphorylated ITAMs recruit Syk kinase, which phosphorylates downstream adaptors) Tj
T*
(   \(LAT, SLP-76\), activating PI3K to generate PI\(3,4,5\)P3 and activating the guanine nucleotide) Tj
T*
(   exchange factors Vav and Tiam1.) Tj
T*
(   3. Pseudopod Extension and Phagosome Closure \(Actin Zipper\):) Tj
T*
(   Vav activates Rac1 and Cdc42, stimulating Arp2/3-driven branched actin polymerization beneath) Tj
T*
(   the plasma membrane. Lamellipodial pseudopods advance around the particle, guided by sequential) Tj
T*
(   receptor-ligand pairing \('zipper mechanism'\), until opposing pseudopod margins fuse,) Tj
T*
(   internalizing the prey into a closed phagosome.) Tj
T*
(   4. Phagolysosome Maturation and Pathogen Killing:) Tj
T*
