(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the role of Septins as a fourth cytoskeletal component in forming diffusion barriers and scaffolding signaling complexes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Septins as the Fourth Cytoskeletal Network:) Tj
T*
(   1. Subunit Structure and Filament Assembly:) Tj
T*
(   Septins are a conserved family of GTP-binding proteins \(classified into four homology groups:) Tj
T*
(   Septin 2, 6, 7, and 9\) that assemble into non-polar, hetero-oligomeric rod-like complexes:) Tj
T*
(   - In mammals, monomers assemble into an octameric rod: [Sept7 - Sept6 - Sept2 - Sept9 - Sept9 -) Tj
T*
(   Sept2 - Sept6 - Sept7].) Tj
T*
(   - Octamers associate end-to-end via their terminal subunits to form continuous non-polar) Tj
T*
(   filaments, rings, and gauze-like meshes along membranes.) Tj
T*
(   2. Membrane Association via Polybasic Domains:) Tj
T*
(   Septin filaments bind directly to curved membranes enriched in acidic phosphoinositides,) Tj
T*
(   specifically PI\(4,5\)P2, via a conserved polybasic motif. They sense and stabilize positive) Tj
T*
(   membrane curvatures.) Tj
T*
(   3. Physiological Functions:) Tj
T*
