12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular pathology of Hutchinson-Gilford Progeria Syndrome \(HGPS\) and how Farnesyltransferase Inhibitors \(FTIs\) provide therapeutic benefit.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Hutchinson-Gilford Progeria Syndrome \(HGPS\) Pathogenesis and FTI Therapy:) Tj
T*
(   1. Normal Biogenesis of Lamin A:) Tj
T*
(   Wild-type Lamin A is encoded by the LMNA gene as a prelamin A precursor containing a C-terminal) Tj
T*
(   CaaX motif \(Cys-Ser-Ile-Met\):) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Protein farnesyltransferase covalently attaches a 15-carbon hydrophobic farnesyl) Tj
T*
(   isoprenoid group to the cysteine.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: The endoprotease Rce1 cleaves the terminal -aaX \(SIM\) tripeptide.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Isoprenylcysteine carboxyl methyltransferase \(ICMT\) methylates the farnesylcysteine) Tj
T*
(   carboxyl group.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4 \(Endoproteolytic Maturation\): The metalloprotease ZMPSTE24 cleaves 15 amino acids from) Tj
T*
(   the C-terminus, including the farnesylated cysteine, releasing mature, unfarnesylated Lamin A) Tj
T*
(   that incorporates into the nuclear lamina.) Tj
T*
(   2. The HGPS Splicing Mutation:) Tj
T*
(   - Caused by a heterozygous silent point mutation in exon 11: c.1824C>T \(p.Gly608Gly\).) Tj
T*
